Replaced the linear scan with a direct-index table: one 16-bit slot per possible message number, holding the position of that message in `mesgs`.

- **Construction:** the table is built once, on the first call, from the constant profile array. Function-local static initialization is thread-safe in C++17.
- **Unchanged behavior:**
  - The table is filled in reverse order, so when two entries share a `num`, the first one wins, as in the original loop.
  - Unknown numbers still return `NULL`.
- **Index fallback:** this assumes `MESGS` is below 65535, so 0xFFFF can mark an empty slot. If `MESGS` were ever larger, the function falls back to the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static const std::vector<FIT_UINT16> table = []()
    {
        std::vector<FIT_UINT16> t(65536, (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF);
        if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
        {
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                t[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
        return t;
    }();

    if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
        if (idx == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[idx];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```

